course: dictionary
lesson: 12188
title: A formal word for sweets
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up but mean something you already know. Confectionery is one of them. «The shop's confectionery aisle was full of gummy bears, toffees and chocolate bars.» The clues are gummy bears, toffees and chocolate, so confectionery must mean sweets and other sugary treats.
explain: Confectionery is a formal word, so you will meet it on signs, in shop aisles and in writing more than in everyday chat. «Mum said we should eat less confectionery to keep our teeth healthy.» The clue is teeth and healthy: here confectionery means sugary foods such as sweets and chocolate.
explain: The word can also name a place. «The old confectionery on the high street still makes its toffee by hand.» The clues are makes and high street, so this confectionery is a shop or factory, not the sweets themselves. Always check: does sweets fit here, or does shop fit better?
fact: Confectionery means sweets, chocolates and similar sugary foods.
fact: As a place, a confectionery is a shop or factory that makes or sells sweets and cakes.
fact: Confectionery is an uncountable noun when it means sweets, so we say «some confectionery», not «a confectionery».
fact: «Confectionery» is a formal word; in the USA people usually say «candy» instead.
fact: In health lessons, we learn to limit confectionery, because sugary foods can harm our teeth.
exercise: «The shop's confectionery section was full of gummy bears and sherbet lemons.» What is confectionery here? | *Sweets and sugary treats | Fruit and vegetables | Toys for the garden | Shoes and clothes
exercise: «Dentists advise us to eat less confectionery to protect our teeth.» Why might a dentist say this? | Teeth need sugar to grow | Confectionery makes your smile brighter | Sweets make teeth stronger | *Sugary sweets can harm teeth
exercise: Which word is a good name for a shop that makes and sells sweets and cakes? | *Confectionery | Shoe shop | Greengrocer | Pet shop
test: «Our family bought a box of chocolates and toffees from the confectionery.» What does confectionery mean here? | Electric toothbrushes | Pushchairs for babies | A bus stop | *A shop that sells sweets and cakes
test: Max says the American word for sweets is «candy». Which is the formal English word that means sweets and sugary foods? | Biscuits | *Confectionery | Porridge | Crisps
exam: Which sentence uses «confectionery» to mean sweets, not a shop? | «She walked past the confectionery on her way home.» | «The confectionery opened at nine o'clock.» | *«Too much confectionery can rot your teeth.»
